-- title:
--   Trivial reduction iff the level-one layer point reduces to the identity
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime with $p\neq 2$, $M$ a positive integer with $p\mid M$ and $p^{2}\nmid M$, and $H\le(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$ a subgroup; the hypothesis `hHp` says that $H$ contains the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}\to(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^{\times}$, so that $\Gamma_H(M)$ sits inside $\Gamma_0(p)$ at the level of the $p$-part. A set $S\subseteq\mathbb N$ of excluded primes is fixed, and `hin : HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H` asserts the two clauses of that predicate: `HeckeInputsHAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for $M,H$ and every prime $\ell$, and, for every $d\in(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$, the existence of an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline F_H=$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`. Here $J_H(M)$ denotes `JH M H`, the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^{0}(\overline{\mathbb Q},\overline F_H)$, and $R(p)=\mathbb Z_{(p)}$ is the subring of rationals with denominator prime to $p$.
--
--   *Hecke data.* A commutative ring $\mathbb T$ is given, an algebra over $\mathbb Z_p$ acting on the Tate module $T_p(J_H(M))$ (the group of sequences $x_n\in J_H(M)$ with $p^{n}x_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$) compatibly with the $\mathbb Z_p$-action; `hfaith` states that $\mathbb T$ acts faithfully on this module, `op` assigns to each generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the generators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q\mid M$, and diamonds $\langle d\rangle$) an element of $\mathbb T$, `hop` says that $\mathrm{op}(g)$ acts on the Tate module as `tateGenOpH M H S p g`, and `hgen` that the $\mathbb Z_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of $\mathrm{op}$ is all of $\mathbb T$. Further, $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb T$ (a finite family of complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals, with $e_i\in\mathfrak m_j$ exactly when $i\neq j$), $i_0$ an index of that splitting, and `hord` requires $\mathrm{op}(U_p)\notin\mathfrak m_{i_0}$.
--
--   *Place, model and Néron object.* $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$, its residue field being algebraically closed of characteristic $p$; `hj` says that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the level-one $q$-expansion function field. $\mathfrak X$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, a flat proper normal integral model of $X_H(M)$ over $\mathbb Z_{(p)}$ carrying a smooth proper model at level $\Gamma_N(p,M,H)$, a geometric curve model $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}$ of $\overline F_H$ with its comparison isomorphism $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{eeta}$, and the remaining data of that structure; $\Lambda$ is a `LevelData p M H hpM Pl`, providing $\sigma_A:\operatorname{Spec}Pl\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z_{(p)}$ with $\mathrm{barPt}(Pl)$ followed by $\sigma_A$ equal to $\mathrm{genPt}(p)$, a scheme with relative group law and dictionaries at the two levels; and $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a smooth separated scheme $O.G$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z_{(p)}$ with relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}:J_H(M)\simeq$ sections of $O.g$ over $\mathrm{genPt}(p)$ compatible with addition, Galois action and Hecke operators, a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, a gluing finset $O.\mathrm{ssFinset}$, and a bijection $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ between the glued Picard group $\mathrm{GluedPic}^{0}$ of the special fibre function field $\mathrm{Fbar}$ and the sections of $O.g$ over $\mathrm{resPt}(Pl)$ followed by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$.
--
--   *Representability and Abel–Jacobi pinning (summarised here, no clause omitted).* `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation formed from $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the unit section of $\Lambda.L$ represents the sub-Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero for the level-$\Gamma_N$ curve with zero section $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak X.\pi$; `hD` asserts the same for $O.G$, $O.g$ and the unit section of $O.L$ over the $\Gamma_M$-model with section $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, and `hDQ` the corresponding statement after base change to $\mathbb Q$, the generic curve being separated by `hsep`. The morphisms $\mathrm{ajQ}$ (an Abel–Jacobi map over $\mathbb Q$), $\mathrm{kQ}$ (a comparison between the fibre over the geometric generic point and the fibre over $\mathbb Q$), $\mathrm{ajbar}:\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C\to O.G$ and the base point $\bar\varepsilon$ are subject to: `hpoinc` (the Poincaré bundle over $\mathbb Q$ is isomorphic to the base change of the one over $\mathbb Z_{(p)}$ pulled back along the first projection), `hajQε` ($\mathrm{ajQ}$ carries the base-changed section to the zero section), `hajQ` (for every field $K$, every $\mathbb Q$-point $t$ of $\operatorname{Spec}K$ and every $K$-point $x$ of the generic curve, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajQ}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section at $t$, i.e. $\mathrm{ajQ}$ classifies $\mathcal O(x-\varepsilon)$), `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂` (compatibility of $\mathrm{kQ}$ with both projections, the second up to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb Q\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$), `hajbar` (the displayed factorisation of $\mathrm{ajbar}$ through $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{eeta}$, $\mathrm{kQ}$, $\mathrm{ajQ}$ and the first projection), `hajbar_over` ($\mathrm{ajbar}$ lies over $\mathrm{genPt}(p)$), `hεbar` and `hεbar_aj` (the point $\bar\varepsilon$ is the one induced by $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, and $\mathrm{ajbar}$ sends it to the identity section), `hpts_law` ($O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law obtained from `hD` and the group cut `algEquivZeroGroupCut`), and `hAJ` (for all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x,s$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ the base point, there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to $[\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,]-[\,\mathrm{place}(s)\,]$ whose class satisfies $(O.\mathrm{pts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }D_v)).1 = x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$).
--
--   *The inertia ring.* $R$ is a commutative domain, Henselian local with algebraically closed residue field, an algebra in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with injective structure map, such that: `hRA` its image lies in $Pl$; `hRdvr` it is a discrete valuation ring; `hRirr` $p$ is irreducible in $R$; `hRfix` an automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ lies in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` precisely when it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise; `hRmax` every element of $Pl$ fixed by the whole inertia subgroup comes from $R$.
--
--   *The finite-part layer.* $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): finite free cocommutative $R$-Hopf algebras $\mathcal G_v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition coalgebra maps whose kernels are the $p^{v}$-torsion ideals. $\Delta$ is an injective additive map from the points $\mathcal G(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ (the direct limit of the convolution groups of $R$-algebra maps $\mathcal G_v\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$) to $J_H(M)$, with: `hΔlev`, for each $v$, $y\in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^{v})$ — the subgroup generated by those $p^{v}$-torsion classes $z$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^{0}(\overline{\mathbb Q},\overline F_H)$ for which $O.\mathrm{pts}(z)$ satisfies `ExtendsToPlace Pl Λ.σA` — if and only if $y$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point; `hΔgal`, $\Delta$ is equivariant for $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-automorphisms over $\mathbb Q$ compatible with $R$-automorphisms; `hΔhecke`, each Hecke generator is realised on the layer by a compatible system of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of $\mathcal G_v$ matching `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$.
--
--   *The quotient layer.* $\mathcal B$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with levelwise $R$-coalgebra maps $\psi_v:\mathcal B_v\to\mathcal G_v$, and $h',$ with: `hhB` $h=O.\mathrm{toricRank}+h_B$; `hhB2` $h_B=2h'$; `hψt` compatibility of $\psi$ with transitions; `hψker` a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal G$ pulls back along $\psi_v$ to the identity point of $\mathcal B$ exactly when $\Delta$ of $x$ lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^{v})$; `hψsurj` every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal B$ arises by pullback along $\psi_v$; `hψred` if the pullback of $x$ along $\psi_v$ reduces to the identity (all values $y^{\sharp}(a)-\varepsilon(a)$ have $Pl$-valuation $<1$) then so does $x$; `hperiod` for each $v$, each $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup, each $p^{v}$-torsion class $z$ and each level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y)=\sigma z-z$, the pullback of $y$ to $\mathcal B$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   *The scheme-level embedding.* A ring map $\rho_h:\mathbb Z_{(p)}\to R$ and morphisms $\iota_v:\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal G_v\to O.G$ are given with: `hρh` compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; `hιbase` $\iota_v$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R\to\mathcal G_v$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$; `hιcl` the induced lift to the base change of $O.G$ to $R$ is a closed immersion; `hιp` $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^{v}$ on $O.L$; `hιpts` $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $x^{\sharp}$ followed by $\iota_v$ for every level-$v$ point $x$; `hιmul` $\iota_v$ transforms convolution of points into the relative group law of $O.L$; `hιt` compatibility with transitions; `hιhecke` each Hecke generator is realised simultaneously on the $\iota_v$ by $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ and on $\Delta$ by `genOpH`; `hιfin` for each $v$ the lift $j_v$ of $\iota_v$ into the pullback over $R$ of the $p^{v}$-kernel of $O.L$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R$ is in the range of $j_v$.
--
--   *Remaining data.* $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\overline F_H$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (a pair of ring automorphisms compatible with the structure map) and `hwgen` says that whenever two $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $y,y'$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ are related by the involution $\mathfrak X.w$, the place of $y'$ is $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ applied to the place of $y$. A ring map $\rho:\mathbb Z_{(p)}\to Pl$ with `hρ` lifting the map to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is given, and `hσA` identifies $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\rho$. Finally `hsp` is a specialisation hypothesis: for each $i\in\{0,1\}$, given $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $y_1,y_2$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$, sections $u_1,u_2$ of the $\Gamma_M$-model over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho$ specialising them and with image in the smooth locus, residue-field points $u_{\kappa,1},u_{\kappa,2}$ of the fibre over the residue field of $Pl$ compatible with the $u_j$ and with the structure map, closed points $P_1,P_2$ of the special-fibre curve $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Mfib}$ mapping to $u_{\kappa,j}$ under the $i$-th component of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{efib}$ followed by $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{comp}$, a degree-zero divisor $D_v=[\mathrm{place}(y_1)]-[\mathrm{place}(y_2)]$, and an admissible gluing datum $x$ whose first component is $[P_1]-[P_2]$ if $i=0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second component is $[P_1]-[P_2]$ if $i=1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit component is $0$ — then there exists a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }D_v)).1=\mathrm{barPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$, and $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the reduction of $s$ equal to the class of $x$ in the glued Picard group.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $x\in J_H(M)$ lying in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$ and every section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ (that is, $s:\operatorname{Spec}Pl\to O.G$ with $s$ followed by $O.g$ equal to $\Lambda.\sigma_A$) such that the generic point $O.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ equals $\mathrm{barPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$, the following two assertions are equivalent:
--
--   (i) the element of the glued Picard group $\mathrm{GluedPic}^{0}$ of the special fibre corresponding under $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ to the reduced section ($\mathrm{resPt}(Pl)$ followed by $s$) is zero;
--
--   (ii) there is a level-one point $y$ of $\mathcal G$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, i.e. an $R$-algebra map $y^{\sharp}:\mathcal G_1\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, whose image under $\Delta$ (via the canonical map from the level-one layer to $\mathcal G(\overline{\mathbb Q})$) is $x$, and which reduces to the identity in the sense that $v_{Pl}\bigl(y^{\sharp}(a)-\varepsilon(a)\bigr)<1$ for every $a\in\mathcal G_1$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit of $\mathcal G_1$ followed by $R\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   This is the dictionary lemma linking the special-fibre description of the Néron object of $J_H(M)$ at a place above $p$ with the $p$-divisible group layer embedded in it: a $p$-torsion class of the finite part has trivial reduction exactly when the corresponding level-one point of the layer reduces to the identity section. It is used by [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.toPic0Pair_ptsSp_symm_atkinLehner_fst_eq_zero_iff_exists_point_reducesToOne_of_mem_corner_of_mem_finPts_bridgePins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.toPic0Pair_ptsSp_symm_atkinLehner_fst_eq_zero_iff_exists_point_reducesToOne_of_mem_corner_of_mem_finPts_bridgePins) in the analysis of the $p$-torsion of $J_H(M)$ at the prime $p$ with $p\parallel M$.
